Like

AssistantAgent(
    tools=[...]
)

So you might ask

Then why MCP?

Excellent question.

AutoGen Tool

Only AutoGen understands it.

AutoGen

↓

Python Function
MCP Tool

Any framework understands it.

AutoGen

Claude

OpenAI

LangGraph

CrewAI

↓

Same MCP Tool

That's why MCP is becoming an industry standard.

9.
Before we code

Until now our flow was:

Supervisor
      │
      ▼
Policy Agent
      │
      ▼
LLM thinks using only prompt

Now it becomes:

Supervisor
      │
      ▼
Policy Agent
      │
      ▼
LLM thinks
      │
      ▼
"I need vendor information."
      │
      ▼
search_vendor()
      │
      ▼
REST API
      │
      ▼
Result
      │
      ▼
LLM continues thinking
      │
      ▼
Final Answer

Notice something important.

The Python code doesn't call the tool.

The LLM decides whether it needs the tool.

That's the whole point of Tool Calling.

10.

************* IMP Learning: ****************
This is actually one of the most important lessons you'll learn in Agentic AI.

What we wanted

We wanted the Policy Agent to behave like this:

Policy Agent

↓

"I need company policy."

↓

Calls get_company_policy()

↓

Gets the result

↓

Thinks using that result

↓

Returns

{
    "status":"FAIL",
    "violations":[...]
}

In simple words:

Use the tool, then continue thinking.

What actually happened

Instead it did this:

Policy Agent

↓

Calls get_company_policy()

↓

Gets the result

↓

Returns the tool result immediately

So instead of giving

{
    "status":"FAIL"
}

it gave

{
    'laptop_limit':10,
    'approved_vendors':['Dell','HP','Lenovo']
}

It basically said

"Here's what the tool returned."

instead of

"I used the tool and here's my decision."

Why did this happen?

Not because your code is wrong.

Not because the prompt is bad.

It's because Groq's implementation of tool calling (with this model and AutoGen) stops after executing the tool instead of continuing to reason.

Think of it like this:

You ask an employee:

"Go ask HR what the leave policy is, then tell me whether John can take leave."

What we expected:

Employee

↓

Asks HR

↓

Gets policy

↓

Reads policy

↓

Comes back

↓

"John can take leave."

What actually happened:

Employee

↓

Asks HR

↓

Gets policy

↓

Hands you the HR document

↓

Walks away 😂

The employee forgot to do the second part.

So what's our solution?

Instead of asking the Policy Agent to fetch the policy...

the Supervisor will fetch it.

Like this:

Supervisor

↓

get_company_policy()

↓

Gets policy

↓

Gives it to Policy Agent

↓

Policy Agent thinks

↓

Returns decision

Now the Policy Agent doesn't have to call the tool.

It simply receives all the information it needs.

Why is this actually better?

Imagine you're a manager.

Would you tell your employee:

"Go to the database, search everything yourself."

or

Would you say:

"Here's the data. Now analyze it."

The second one is how most real enterprise AI systems work.

One sentence to remember

The problem wasn't the tool. The problem was that the model returned the tool's output instead of using the tool's output to continue reasoning.

So we changed the architecture:

❌ Agent fetches data and reasons

↓

✅ Supervisor fetches data, Agent only reasons

I actually like this solution better because it teaches a very important architectural principle:

LLMs are good at reasoning. Python is good at fetching data. Let each do what it's best at.


